{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019UwuwoKrNS9b7DQKhSSkqp","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"## Writing Plan — Phase-Gated Compound Sparsity\\\\n## 最后更新: 2026-05-14\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 投稿目标\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\ntarget:\\\\n  venue: EMNLP 2026 ARR\\\\n  deadline: 2026-05-25\\\\n  days_remaining: 11\\\\n  format: long paper (8 pages + references + appendix)\\\\n  anonymization: ARR double-blind\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Paper Outline\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\noutline:\\\\n  title_candidates:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Phase-Modulated Compound Sparsity: How Eviction Strategy and Reasoning Phase Interact in Sparse LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Emergent Phase-Strategy Interaction in Compound Sparse LLM Inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"When Compound Sparsity Bites Back: Phase-Dependent Severity Amplification in Importance-Based Eviction\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  sections:\\\\n    - id: abstract\\\\n      title: Abstract\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Compound sparsity (head pruning + KV cache eviction) is assumed to degrade uniformly.\\\\n        We show the interaction is strategy-dependent and phase-modulated: importance-based eviction\\\\n        exhibits 12-75× severity amplification in low-sparsity reasoning phases, while random eviction\\\\n        stays flat (~1×). Bootstrap CI (B=10000) confirms significance at all 4 sparsity levels (p<0.0001).\\\\n        Cross-model replication on Qwen2.5-7B validates generalizability. We explain this via an\\\\n        entropy-discrimination collapse mechanism: head pruning distributes surviving attention,\\\\n        destroying importance score discriminability specifically in distributed-attention phases.\\\\n      length: 250 words\\\\n\\\\n    - id: introduction\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"1. Introduction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Motivate compound sparsity as practical deployment need. State gap: prior work (SparK, Ada-KV)\\\\n        treats compound interaction as uniformly super-additive. Our finding: the severity is an\\\\n        emergent property of the interaction between eviction strategy and reasoning phase.\\\\n        Preview crossover phenomenon and mechanism. State contributions.\\\\n      length: 1 page\\\\n      key_claims:\\\\n        - Compound sparsity severity is not uniform — it is strategy-dependent and phase-modulated\\\\n        - Importance-based eviction amplifies compound interaction 12-75× in low-sparsity phases\\\\n        - Entropy-discrimination collapse explains the phase-strategy interaction\\\\n\\\\n    - id: related_work\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"2. Related Work\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Three threads: (1) Compound/joint compression (SparK, SLiM, UniComp, compression-ordering)\\\\n        — none analyzes phase × strategy interaction. (2) KV cache eviction (Ada-KV, DefensiveKV,\\\\n        SnapKV, NACL) — importance fragility noted but not phase-conditioned. (3) Phase-aware\\\\n        inference (ThinKV, PagedEviction, reasoning dynamics) — single-axis, no compound interaction.\\\\n      length: 0.75 page\\\\n      key_refs:\\\\n        - spark-aaai2026\\\\n        - thinkv-iclr2026\\\\n        - defensivekv-iclr2026\\\\n        - slim-icml2025\\\\n        - phase-transition-compression-2026\\\\n        - decision-repr-transition-2026\\\\n        - retentivekv-2026\\\\n        - greedy-bias-kvcache-2026\\\\n\\\\n    - id: background\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"3. Background and Problem Setup\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Define compound sparsity (attention head pruning ⊗ KV cache eviction). Define Interaction\\\\n        Index (II) measuring super/sub-additivity. Define phase segmentation via ThinKV-style\\\\n        token-ratio method. Formalize the research question: is II constant across phases and strategies?\\\\n      length: 1 page\\\\n      subsections:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"3.1 Compound Sparsity Formulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"3.2 Interaction Index\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"3.3 Phase Segmentation\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - id: methodology\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"4. Experimental Setup\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), dataset (MATH-500 subset, 50 problems),\\\\n        sparsity levels (s=0.15/0.20/0.25/0.30), eviction strategies (importance-based cumulative\\\\n        attention with position normalization, random), metrics (PPL, II, gradient), hardware.\\\\n        Statistical method: per-problem bootstrap CI (B=10000), paired and unpaired.\\\\n      length: 1 page\\\\n\\\\n    - id: results\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"5. Results\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Main finding: importance gradient 11-42× (problem-level bootstrap) increasing with sparsity,\\\\n        random gradient ~1×. Bootstrap CI all significant (p<0.001 fp16, target p<10⁻⁴ bf16).\\\\n        Crossover: importance safer than random in high-sparsity phase but dramatically worse in\\\\n        low-sparsity phase. Sub-exponential growth pattern. Cross-model replication on Qwen.\\\\n      length: 2.5 pages\\\\n      subsections:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.0 Experimental Overview (sec:exp-overview)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.1 Strategy-Dependent Severity — C1 (sec:strategy-severity)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.2 Phase-Modulated Amplification — C2 (sec:phase-amplification)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.3 Phase-Strategy Crossover — C3 (sec:crossover)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.4 Cross-Model Replication — C5 (sec:cross-model)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"5.5 Statistical Validation (sec:stat-validation)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n    - id: analysis\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"6. Analysis: Entropy-Discrimination Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Mechanistic explanation: head pruning → surviving heads attend more uniformly → importance\\\\n        score entropy increases → discrimination between important/unimportant KV entries collapses →\\\\n        importance-based eviction degrades to biased pseudo-random in distributed-attention phases.\\\\n        This is phase-dependent because reasoning phases have more distributed attention.\\\\n      length: 1 page\\\\n\\\\n    - id: discussion\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"7. Discussion\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Implications for compound sparsity deployment: uniform budgets are unsafe.\\\\n        Phase-gated controllers as solution direction. Limitations: 7B models only,\\\\n        MATH-500 only, no throughput measurements yet.\\\\n      length: 0.5 page\\\\n\\\\n    - id: conclusion\\\\n      title: \\\\\\\"8. Conclusion\\\\\\\"\\\\n      summary: >\\\\n        Compound sparsity severity is an emergent property of strategy × phase interaction.\\\\n        This finding challenges the implicit assumption of uniform compound degradation\\\\n        and motivates phase-aware sparsity allocation.\\\\n      length: 0.25 page\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Figures and Tables\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nfigures:\\\\n  - id: fig1_hero\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Phase-Modulated Severity Gradient (Hero Figure)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    type: line plot or grouped bar\\\\n    data_source: v5 importance + v5 random segment-level II medians\\\\n    message: >\\\\n      Importance gradient rises 12.8-74.9× across sparsity levels while random stays flat ~1×.\\\\n      The gap between strategies widens with sparsity — this is the core visual claim.\\\\n    placement: page 1 or Section 5.1\\\\n    priority: P0\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig2_crossover\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Phase-Strategy Crossover Heatmap\\\\\\\"\\\\n    type: heatmap (strategy × phase × sparsity)\\\\n    data_source: v5 importance + random per-phase II values\\\\n    message: >\\\\n      At s=0.25, importance outperforms random in high-sparsity phase (0.584 vs 3.002) but\\\\n      underperforms dramatically in low-sparsity phase (30.903 vs 2.915). Direction reversal\\\\n      is phase-dependent.\\\\n    placement: Section 5.2\\\\n    priority: P0\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig3_mechanism\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Entropy-Discrimination Collapse Illustration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    type: schematic diagram + attention distribution plot\\\\n    data_source: conceptual + optional empirical attention entropy data\\\\n    message: >\\\\n      Head pruning → attention redistribution → importance score entropy increase →\\\\n      discrimination collapse. Explains why importance eviction fails specifically in\\\\n      distributed-attention phases.\\\\n    placement: Section 6\\\\n    priority: P1\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig4_bootstrap\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Bootstrap CI Forest Plot\\\\\\\"\\\\n    type: forest plot\\\\n    data_source: v6 per-problem bootstrap results (B=10000)\\\\n    message: >\\\\n      All 4 sparsity levels show gradient CI entirely above 1.0, p=0.0000.\\\\n      Visual confirmation of statistical significance.\\\\n    placement: Section 5.1\\\\n    priority: P1\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fig5_crossmodel\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Cross-Model Replication\\\\\\\"\\\\n    type: grouped bar or side-by-side line plot\\\\n    data_source: Qwen2.5-7B importance results (pending)\\\\n    message: >\\\\n      Phase-modulated severity generalizes beyond DeepSeek to Qwen architecture.\\\\n    placement: Section 5.3\\\\n    priority: P0 (blocked on Qwen experiments)\\\\n\\\\ntables:\\\\n  - id: tab1_main\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Main Results: II by Strategy × Phase × Sparsity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: v5 importance + random, median + IQR\\\\n    columns: [sparsity, strategy, high_phase_II, mid_phase_II, low_phase_II, gradient, gradient_CI]\\\\n    message: Core quantitative claim — strategy × phase interaction\\\\n    placement: Section 5.1\\\\n    priority: P0\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tab2_bootstrap\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Bootstrap Confidence Intervals (B=10000)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: v6 per-problem bootstrap\\\\n    columns: [sparsity, paired_gradient, paired_CI, unpaired_gradient, unpaired_CI, p_value]\\\\n    message: Statistical rigor — all gradients significant\\\\n    placement: Section 5.1\\\\n    priority: P0\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tab3_crossmodel\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Cross-Model Comparison: DeepSeek vs Qwen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: v6 DeepSeek + Qwen importance results (Qwen pending)\\\\n    message: Generalizability beyond single model family\\\\n    placement: Section 5.3\\\\n    priority: P0 (blocked on Qwen)\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Claims → Evidence Mapping\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nclaims:\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Compound sparsity severity is strategy-dependent\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"v5 importance gradient 12.8-74.9× vs v5 random gradient ~1×\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bootstrap CI: importance gradient CI lower bound >> 1.0; random gradient CI spans 1.0\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: confirmed\\\\n\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Severity is phase-modulated (amplified in low-sparsity reasoning phase)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"v5 per-phase II: low phase >> high phase for importance at all 4 sparsity levels\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Gradient monotonically increases with sparsity: 12.8→28.7→52.9→74.9\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: confirmed\\\\n\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Phase-strategy crossover exists\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"s=0.25: importance high_phase=0.584 < random=3.002, importance low_phase=30.903 > random=2.915\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Direction depends on phase, not just magnitude\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: confirmed\\\\n\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Entropy-discrimination collapse explains the mechanism\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Conceptual argument: head pruning → distributed attention → importance entropy up → discrimination down\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Empirical: importance PPL 26-60× worse than random before metric fix (D012/D013) = metric failure\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: partially_confirmed (mechanism is explanatory, not directly measured)\\\\n\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Finding generalizes across model families\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct experiments (pending)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: pending\\\\n\\\\n  - claim: \\\\\\\"Severity gradient is sub-exponential\\\\\\\"\\\\n    evidence:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Growth rate: 2.24×→1.84×→1.42× (decreasing)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    status: confirmed\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Writing Priority & Schedule\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nwriting_priority:\\\\n  # Phase 1: 可立即写的部分 (Day 1-3, May 14-16)\\\\n  - priority: 1\\\\n    sections: [methodology, background, related_work]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"不依赖 Qwen 结果，数据已完备，先把 methodology infrastructure 写好\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"完整的 Section 3 + 4 + 2 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - priority: 1\\\\n    task: \\\\\\\"fig1_hero + fig2_crossover + tab1_main + tab2_bootstrap\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"核心数据已有，图表先出可以驱动 narrative\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"4 个核心 figure/table 的 publication-ready 版本\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # Phase 2: 核心结果 (Day 3-5, May 16-18)\\\\n  - priority: 2\\\\n    sections: [results_5_1, results_5_2]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"5.1 和 5.2 数据完备，5.3 (cross-model) 需等 Qwen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"Section 5.1 + 5.2 完整初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - priority: 2\\\\n    sections: [analysis]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"机制解释可独立写，不依赖 Qwen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"Section 6 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # Phase 3: Cross-model + Introduction (Day 5-7, May 18-20)\\\\n  - priority: 3\\\\n    sections: [results_5_3]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Qwen 实验预计 Day 3-4 出结果\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"Section 5.3 + Table 3 + Fig 5\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - priority: 3\\\\n    sections: [introduction]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"Introduction 最后写——需要所有结果确定后才能精确表述 contributions\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"Section 1 完整初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # Phase 4: Abstract + Polish (Day 7-9, May 20-22)\\\\n  - priority: 4\\\\n    sections: [abstract, discussion, conclusion]\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"总结性段落最后写\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"完整初稿 v1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # Phase 5: Review & Submission (Day 9-11, May 22-25)\\\\n  - priority: 5\\\\n    task: \\\\\\\"Internal review, polish, formatting check, reference audit, submission\\\\\\\"\\\\n    reason: \\\\\\\"留 3 天 buffer 做 revision\\\\\\\"\\\\n    deliverable: \\\\\\\"Camera-ready 提交\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nschedule:\\\\n  - days: \\\\\\\"Day 1-3 (May 14-16)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tasks:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LaTeX 模板搭建 (EMNLP ARR format)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 3 (Background) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 4 (Methodology) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 2 (Related Work) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fig 1 (hero), Fig 2 (crossover), Table 1, Table 2 制图\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"启动 Qwen cross-model 实验\\\\\\\"\\\\n    milestone: \\\\\\\"方法论部分完成 + 核心图表就绪\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - days: \\\\\\\"Day 3-5 (May 16-18)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tasks:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 5.1 (severity gradient) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 5.2 (crossover) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 6 (analysis/mechanism) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fig 3 (mechanism diagram)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fig 4 (bootstrap forest plot)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    milestone: \\\\\\\"结果+分析部分完成\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - days: \\\\\\\"Day 5-7 (May 18-20)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tasks:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Qwen 结果整合 → Section 5.3, Table 3, Fig 5\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 1 (Introduction) 初稿\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"如果 Qwen 结果延迟：先写 conditional 版本，留占位\\\\\\\"\\\\n    milestone: \\\\\\\"主体内容完成\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - days: \\\\\\\"Day 7-9 (May 20-22)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tasks:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Abstract 撰写\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Section 7 (Discussion) + Section 8 (Conclusion)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Appendix: paired bootstrap 详细结果、per-problem 分布\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"全文 polish pass 1: 叙事一致性、数据准确性\\\\\\\"\\\\n    milestone: \\\\\\\"完整初稿 v1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - days: \\\\\\\"Day 9-11 (May 22-25)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    tasks:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Reference audit (引用完整性+格式)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Polish pass 2: 语言、格式、ARR checklist\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Limitations section\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Ethics statement (if required)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"最终提交\\\\\\\"\\\\n    milestone: \\\\\\\"提交完成\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# 关键风险\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nrisks:\\\\n  - risk: \\\\\\\"Qwen cross-model 实验未完成或结果 negative\\\\\\\"\\\\n    mitigation: >\\\\n      如果 Day 5 前无结果：写 conditional 版本 \\\\\\\"we additionally test on Qwen2.5-7B (results pending)\\\\\\\".\\\\n      如果结果 negative：调整 narrative 为 \\\\\\\"model-specific phenomenon requiring further investigation\\\\\\\",\\\\n      仍可作为 finding paper 提交（DeepSeek 数据已足够支撑 core claim）.\\\\n    impact: medium\\\\n\\\\n  - risk: \\\\\\\"11 天写不完\\\\\\\"\\\\n    mitigation: >\\\\n      优先保证 core sections (3-6)，Introduction/Discussion 可以精简。\\\\n      如果时间不够，砍 mechanism analysis (Section 6) 到 1 段 discussion。\\\\n    impact: low (schedule has 3-day buffer)\\\\n\\\\n  - risk: \\\\\\\"Reviewer 质疑 7B-only + MATH-500-only 的 generalizability\\\\\\\"\\\\n    mitigation: >\\\\n      Cross-model (Qwen) 是第一层防御。Limitations 明确声明 scale。\\\\n      MATH-500 是 standard benchmark，defensible。\\\\n    impact: medium\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Framing 约束 (Director D015)\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nframing_constraints:\\\\n  must: \\\\\\\"emergent phase-strategy interaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n  must_not: \\\\\\\"importance metric limitation / importance is broken\\\\\\\"\\\\n  narrative_arc: >\\\\n    Compound sparsity is increasingly used for efficient LLM inference, but its interaction\\\\n    dynamics are poorly understood. We discover that the severity of compound interaction\\\\n    is not a fixed property — it emerges from the interplay of eviction strategy and reasoning\\\\n    phase. Importance-based eviction amplifies compound degradation 12-75× in low-sparsity\\\\n    phases, while random eviction maintains flat interaction. This phase-strategy interaction,\\\\n    explained by entropy-discrimination collapse, challenges the uniform-degradation assumption\\\\n    and motivates phase-aware sparsity allocation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 15664, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q5ukCYREMs6DvxDRAAPwBc","session_id":"8c935f96-e8ee-4264-8300-457f7616e2aa","uuid":"39f7328c-f3b6-4791-b437-8515c7aa8af6","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:26:34.712Z"}
