{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RJv33iKGsLhN2eCBK8bK35","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Compound Sparsity Formulation}\n2\t\\label{app:formulation-details}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Attention head pruning.}\n5\tHeads are ranked by Frobenius norm importance $w_j^{(\\ell)} = \\|\\mathbf{A}_j^{(\\ell)}\\|_F$ \\cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}; a binary mask $m_j^{(\\ell)} \\in \\{0,1\\}$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\\cdot H$ heads:\n6\t\\begin{equation}\n7\t    \\tilde{\\mathbf{o}}_j^{(\\ell)} = m_j^{(\\ell)} \\cdot \\mathbf{o}_j^{(\\ell)}.\n8\t    \\label{eq:head-mask}\n9\t\\end{equation}\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{KV cache eviction.}\n12\tA position mask $\\mathbf{m}_k \\in \\{0,1\\}^T$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\\cdot T$ positions (BOS always preserved):\n13\t\\begin{equation}\n14\t    \\tilde{a}_{ij} = a_{ij} \\cdot m_{k,j}, \\quad \\forall\\, i, j.\n15\t    \\label{eq:kv-mask}\n16\t\\end{equation}\n17\tUnder importance-based eviction, each position $j$ is scored by its cumulative attention mass with position normalization:\n18\t\\begin{equation}\n19\t    \\mathrm{imp}(j) = \\frac{\\sum_{\\ell, h, i} a_{ij}^{(\\ell,h)}}{T - j},\n20\t    \\label{eq:kv-importance}\n21\t\\end{equation}\n22\twhere the denominator compensates for causal bias---earlier positions are attended to by more queries \\cite{thinkv-iclr2026, defensivekv-iclr2026}. Under random eviction, positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random.\n23\t\n24\t\\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}\n25\tApplying both mechanisms simultaneously yields compound sparsity. The two axes interact: head pruning redistributes attention mass, altering the importance ranking for KV eviction. Prior work \\cite{spark-aaai2026} reports super-additive joint effects but treats interaction magnitude as a fixed property of $s$.\n26\t\n27\t\\paragraph{Attention sparsity metric.}\n28\tFor each query position $i$ and head $h$, we compute the fraction of key positions receiving negligible attention:\n29\t\\begin{equation}\n30\t    \\sigma_i^{(h)} = \\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{j=1}^{T} \\mathbf{1}\\!\\left[a_{ij}^{(h)} < 0.01 \\cdot \\max_k a_{ik}^{(h)}\\right].\n31\t    \\label{eq:attn-sparsity}\n32\t\\end{equation}\n33\tThe per-position sparsity $\\sigma_i = \\frac{1}{H}\\sum_h \\sigma_i^{(h)}$ averages across all heads. Tokens are partitioned into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity phases by the tercile boundaries of $\\{\\sigma_i\\}$; segments shorter than 10 tokens are merged into adjacent phases.\n34\t\n35\t\\section{Multiplicative Null Verification}\n36\t\\label{app:multiplicative-null}\n37\t\n38\tWe verified that replacing the additive null $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (Eq.~\\ref{eq:ii}) with the multiplicative null $r_b = r_a \\cdot r_k$ does not change qualitative conclusions. Under the multiplicative formulation, the interaction index becomes $\\mathrm{II}_{\\times} = r_b - r_a \\cdot r_k$. Notably, $\\mathrm{II}_{\\times} < 0$ across all phases and sparsity levels: the compound interaction is \\emph{sub-multiplicative} everywhere---actual compound degradation is less severe than the multiplicative prediction. $G_{\\times}$ therefore quantifies the ratio of sub-multiplicative deviations between phases, not super-multiplicative amplification: a value of $G_{\\times}{=}30$ means the low-sparsity phase deviates from the multiplicative null by $30{\\times}$ more than the high-sparsity phase. Under importance-based eviction, $G_{\\times}{=}21.1$--$36.2{\\times}$ (DeepSeek) and $43.9$--$64.0{\\times}$ (Qwen) across $s \\in \\{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\\}$, yielding a combined range of $21$--$64{\\times}$. Although compound effects do not exceed the multiplicative prediction in any phase, the low-sparsity phase's deviation from the multiplicative null is $21$--$64{\\times}$ larger than the high-sparsity phase's, indicating that the phase-gated severity gradient is preserved under the multiplicative null. These $n{=}500$ exact values are more conservative than the preliminary $n{=}50$ calibrated estimates ($\\approx$51--183${\\times}$), reflecting reduced variance at full sample size. Values are computed using aggregate per-phase PPL ratios; per-problem exact computation is left for future work. Random eviction remains flat ($G_{\\times} \\approx 1$). The crossover at $s{=}0.25$ is preserved. We adopt the additive formulation in the main text as the more conservative baseline: it assumes zero interaction (compound loss equals the sum of individual losses), so rejection ($\\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. The qualitative conclusions---phase-modulated severity, strategy asymmetry, and crossover---are preserved under the multiplicative null.\n39\t\n40\t% Bootstrap and permutation details moved to main text \\S\\ref{sec:stat-validation}\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q5ukCYREMs6DvxDRAAPwBc","session_id":"8c935f96-e8ee-4264-8300-457f7616e2aa","uuid":"ff7f52ee-1d4c-4af1-8114-1698f28d7e58","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:26:51.009Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018fGZUqwLGZVh19iqJFvtBk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DP5pVFyQJ18cLMD9yDGW1s","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of an EMNLP submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ACL Rolling Review / EMNLP)\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5)**: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Accept, 3=Findings track, 2=Resubmit with revisions, 1=Reject\n**Soundness (1-5)**: 5=Excellent/most thorough, 4=Strong/sufficient support for all claims, 3=Acceptable/main claims supported, 2=Poor/main claims unsupported, 1=Major issues\n**Excitement (1-5)**: 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially interesting, 1=Not exciting\n**Reproducibility (1-5)**\n**Confidence (1-5)**\n\nHalf-point increments allowed. EMNLP acceptance rate ~22-25%.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Reasoning Phase Modulates the Severity of Pruning–Eviction Interactions in 7B LLMs\"\n\n**Core Contributions Claimed**:\n1. Phase gradient is robust: monotonic dose-response between sparsity and phase gradient magnitude, Spearman ρ=1.0 across 3 benchmarks, 3 models\n2. Strategy specificity is task- and architecture-modulated (not universal): imp/rand ratio decays from 15× (MATH-500) to 2.1× (GPQA)\n3. Discrimination collapse mechanism: Gini coefficient decline explains phase gradient\n\n**Related Work discussed (§5)**:\n- **SparK [2025]**: Joint pruning + quantization, treats interaction as uniform. Paper claims to show it's phase-dependent.\n- **ThinKV [2025]**: Phase-aware KV compression (single-axis). Paper claims to add compound (dual-axis) dimension.\n- **R-KV [2025]**: Reasoning-aware KV compression (single-axis). Same distinction.\n- **Physics of KV Cache Compression [2026]**: Phase transitions in compression-rate dimension. Paper explicitly distinguishes: their \"phase\" = critical points in compression rate; this paper's \"phase\" = temporal reasoning segments. Claims orthogonality.\n- **HIES [2025]**: Entropy-compensated importance scoring. Paper claims external validation for discrimination collapse mechanism.\n- **Lethe [AAAI 2026]**: Layer-time adaptive KV pruning. Mentioned in references but not discussed in related work.\n\n---\n\n## Full Paper Content (7 pages)\n\n**Abstract**: Compound sparsity (head pruning + KV eviction) interaction severity is phase-modulated — amplified in later reasoning phases. Across 3 benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA) and 3 7B models (2 architectures), importance-based eviction shows monotonic dose-response (Spearman ρ=1.0, p=0.042). Strategy specificity is task/architecture-modulated (15× to 2.1×). Discrimination collapse (Gini decline) is the proposed mechanism.\n\n**Key experimental results**:\n- GPQA dose-response: Table 1 shows monotonic increase in phase gradient with sparsity (d_z 0.52→0.91)\n- Strategy specificity: imp/rand ratio at s=0.30 is ~2.1× on GPQA, continuing decay from MATH-500 (15-36×) → GSM8K (2.3-3.8×)\n- LLaMA n=250: 2/4 sparsities significant for imp_vs_rand, small effects (d_z 0.21-0.33), but within-importance gradient strong (d_z≈1.0-1.1)\n- Random on LLaMA shows non-trivial phase gradients (d_z=0.36-0.44)\n- Cross-benchmark at s=0.30: MATH-500 8.48, GSM8K 4.68, GPQA 3.68 — all monotonic\n\n**Discussion**: Discrimination collapse as primary mechanism, entropy redistribution as secondary/model-dependent. Task dependence correlated with reasoning structure regularity. GQA reduces strategy×phase interaction but maintains within-importance gradient.\n\n**Limitations**: 7B only, varying strategy specificity, failed mitigation pilot, fixed tertile segmentation.\n\n---\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions in detail:\n\n### A. Novelty Assessment\n1. What is genuinely new here? Is \"compound interaction is phase-modulated\" a sufficiently novel finding?\n2. How incremental is this over: (a) ThinKV's single-axis phase-awareness, (b) Physics of KV Cache's phase transitions, (c) HIES's importance score collapse?\n3. The paper seems to combine known ingredients (phase segmentation from ThinKV + compound effects from SparK + score collapse from HIES) and measure their intersection. Is the intersection itself novel enough?\n4. The \"discrimination collapse\" mechanism — is this a genuine mechanistic insight or a re-description of HIES in a compound setting?\n5. Does the paper generate new knowledge that changes how practitioners think about compound compression?\n\n### B. Related Work Completeness & Positioning\n1. Is the related work section comprehensive? What important works are missing?\n2. Is the distinction from \"Physics of KV Cache Compression\" convincing? Both use \"phase\" prominently — will readers be confused?\n3. Is Lethe [AAAI 2026] adequately discussed (it's only in references, not in the related work text)?\n4. Are there missing references to: attention head pruning methods, KV cache compression surveys, reasoning-aware inference optimization?\n5. Only 6 references total — is this adequate for an EMNLP submission?\n\n### C. Contribution Framing\n1. Is the title accurate? \"Phase-Gated\" vs. \"Phase-Dependent\" vs. \"Phase-Modulated\" — the title says \"Phase-Dependent\" but the phenomenon described is continuous/graded, not gated.\n2. Does the abstract oversell? The abstract says \"phase-modulated\" and qualifies strategy specificity, which seems honest. But \"implications for phase-aware deployment strategies\" when the mitigation pilot failed is questionable.\n3. The three claimed contributions: are they three separate contributions or one finding (phase gradient) with two qualifications (task-dependent, architecture-dependent)?\n4. \"Challenge the implicit assumption that compound degradation distributes uniformly\" — who actually makes this assumption? Is the strawman valid?\n\n### D. Significance & Impact\n1. Who benefits from this work? Practitioners deploying compound compression? Researchers studying inference efficiency?\n2. Without actionable mitigation (pilot failed), is this purely diagnostic? Is diagnostic work publishable at EMNLP?\n3. Does this open new research directions or close them?\n\n### E. Specific Concerns About Claims Scope\n1. Strategy specificity decays from 15× to 2.1× — at what point does this stop being \"strategy-specific\" and start being \"strategy-irrelevant\"?\n2. LLaMA random also shows phase gradients — does this undermine the \"importance-based eviction amplifies\" framing? Maybe the phenomenon is just \"later phases are more fragile regardless of strategy.\"\n3. 7B-only: the paper says \"theoretically scale-independent\" for discrimination collapse — is this a legitimate argument or hand-waving?\n4. The crossover phenomenon (mentioned in review_context as \"genuinely novel\" by simulated R3) — is this actually discussed in the paper? I don't see explicit crossover analysis.\n\n---\n\n**Output Format**: Provide a structured review with:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Scores: Overall, Soundness, Excitement, Reproducibility, Confidence (use ARR 1-5 scale, half-points allowed)\n6. Positioning suggestions (how to sharpen the contribution)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":785,"cache_read_input_tokens":50540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":785},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c935f96-e8ee-4264-8300-457f7616e2aa","uuid":"c3f477ed-59cf-4228-acb5-d2159435ab5b"}
