{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMtkbYVMZAkiGmFexDBmbU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{array}\n13\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{Emergent Phase-Strategy Interaction in Compound Sparsity:\\\\Severity Is Not a Constant}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tCompound sparsity---jointly applying attention head pruning and KV cache eviction---is a practical strategy for efficient LLM inference, yet prior work treats the resulting compound degradation as uniformly super-additive.\n26\tWe discover that compound severity is an \\emph{emergent property} of the interaction between eviction strategy and reasoning phase.\n27\tUsing a within-problem paired diagnostic on three models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) across two architectures and three benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond), we show that importance-based eviction produces a monotonically increasing compound interaction severity across reasoning phases (paired median difference: $0.39 \\!\\to\\! 8.48$ on DeepSeek/MATH-500), while random masking yields a dramatically smaller gradient ($2.7$--$15\\times$ lower).\n28\tPhase-strategy interaction generalizes across three models and two architectures, with architecture-dependent magnitude ($2$--$3\\times$ on LLaMA vs.\\ $15$--$38\\times$ on Qwen2/DeepSeek).\n29\tCross-benchmark replication on GPQA Diamond confirms strategy specificity at $13\\times$ importance/random ratio on graduate-level reasoning, eliminating task-specific artifact concerns.\n30\tWe attribute this phenomenon to \\emph{discrimination collapse}: head pruning degrades the discriminative power of importance scores (Gini coefficient decline consistent across models), causing importance-based eviction to degenerate into biased pseudo-random selection in later reasoning phases.\n31\tOur findings challenge the uniform-degradation assumption and motivate phase-aware compound compression.\n32\t\\end{abstract}\n33\t\n34\t% ============================================================\n35\t\\section{Introduction}\n36\t\\label{sec:intro}\n37\t\n38\tDeploying large language models (LLMs) under resource constraints often requires combining multiple compression axes---for instance, pruning attention heads to reduce computation while evicting KV cache entries to cap memory~\\citep{spark2024,slim2024}.\n39\tThis \\emph{compound sparsity} setting is increasingly common yet poorly understood: prior methods either optimize each axis independently or treat their interaction as a fixed super-additive penalty~\\citep{spark2024,adakv2024}.\n40\t\n41\tWe challenge this uniform-degradation assumption.\n42\tThrough a controlled diagnostic study on reasoning tasks, we find that the severity of compound interaction is not a constant---it is an \\emph{emergent property} of the eviction strategy and the reasoning phase in which it operates.\n43\tSpecifically, importance-based KV eviction under head pruning produces compound interaction that amplifies monotonically as reasoning deepens (low-sparsity phases), while random masking shows a dramatically weaker phase gradient.\n44\t\n45\tOur contributions are:\n46\t\\begin{enumerate}\n47\t\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery.} We identify the strategy $\\times$ phase interaction in compound sparsity severity, quantified via per-problem paired diagnostics with paired median differences ranging from $0.39$ to $8.48$ (DeepSeek, MATH-500) and Cohen's $d_z$ from $0.61$ to $1.13$.\n48\t\\item \\textbf{Multi-dimensional generalization.} We replicate the finding across three models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B), two architectures (Qwen2 GQA, LLaMA MHA), and three benchmarks (MATH-500, GSM8K, GPQA Diamond), establishing a five-layer generalization chain.\n49\t\\item \\textbf{Mechanistic explanation.} We propose \\emph{discrimination collapse}---head pruning degrades the Gini coefficient and coefficient of variation of importance scores, causing importance-based eviction to lose selectivity---as the primary mechanism, with entropy redistribution as a model-dependent secondary factor.\n50\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DP5pVFyQJ18cLMD9yDGW1s","session_id":"8c935f96-e8ee-4264-8300-457f7616e2aa","uuid":"5f94ae75-e653-4059-bcca-761bf7fb37e2","timestamp":"2026-05-16T17:27:45.476Z"}
